HYP-2721/S70 q0 Vitali-cone minimum tax LP
Normalize q0=1; minimize sum_{t>0}|q_t| while preserving W_1..W_r.

  r  min_tax        support q_t                         local_B_tax
  0  1 (1.000000000)    q1=-1                                         1 (1.000000000)
  1  7/5 (1.400000000)  q1=-6/5 q6=1/5                                3 (3.000000000)
      cheaper than local finite difference B_2: q1=-2 q2=1
  2  3 (3.000000000)    q1=-9/5 q3=1 q6=-1/5                          7 (7.000000000)
      cheaper than local finite difference B_3: q1=3 q2=-3 q3=1
  3  7 (7.000000000)    q1=-3 q2=5/2 q5=-1 q6=1/2                     15 (15.000000000)
      cheaper than local finite difference B_4: q1=-4 q2=6 q3=-4 q4=1
  4  19 (19.000000000)  q1=-9/2 q2=15/2 q3=-5 q5=3/2 q6=-1/2          31 (31.000000000)
      cheaper than local finite difference B_5: q1=5 q2=-10 q3=10 q4=-5 q5=1
  5  63 (63.000000000)  q1=-6 q2=15 q3=-20 q4=15 q5=-6 q6=1           63 (63.000000000)

SYNTHESIS
  The local finite-difference B_{r+1} is the canonical hidden move, but
  it is not always the cheapest abstract cone move once high t atoms are
  allowed.  High missed-count atoms can cancel low factorial moments with
  less non-origin L1 tax.  Therefore a proof cannot use the abstract atom
  cone alone: it must retain generated-word compatibility and high-tail
  Bonferroni/transfer constraints before evaluating Q0.
